<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paolo Uccello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a>–<a href="/years/1475">1475</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paolo Uccello (1397–1475) was a Florentine painter known for his fascination with linear perspective. His works include the painted equestrian monument to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hawkwood">John Hawkwood</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a> of 1436 and the three panels of the Battle of San Romano, now divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in which foreshortened figures and broken lances show his experiments with depth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paolo-uccello.jpg" alt="Paolo Uccello" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florentine School, Unknown Master, Italian (active late 15th century); généralement attribuée à Paolo Uccello · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cinq_ma%C3%AEtres_de_la_Renaissance_florentine_-_Uccello_(Louvre).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
